{"step_index":16,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:06:44Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nRULE 1 Remove these type of lines you have inside your text for each of every pages, because I have copied them.---Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 1 of 23                                                               RULE 2 There are flowcharts in which arrows are present and signs are present of plus minus. You don't have to remove them. You can just give in continuous format as it is. Don't remove them.--------------------------------Here is the text---------              1. OSTEOMYELITIS — ACUTE AND CHRONIC Definition Osteomyelitis is an inflammatory and infective disease of bone and bone marrow, usually caused by microorganisms, most commonly bacteria, resulting in destruction of bone and surrounding tissues. Acute osteomyelitis: Infection of bone of relatively recent onset, usually associated with acute inflammation and systemic manifestations. Chronic osteomyelitis: Persistent or recurrent bone infection lasting for a prolonged period, usually associated with necrotic bone (sequestrum), new bone formation (involucrum), sinus formation and recurrent discharge. Common Causative Organism Staphylococcus aureus is the most common organism. Other organisms: • • • • • Streptococci Gram-negative bacilli Pseudomonas aeruginosa Salmonella — particularly in sickle-cell disease Mycobacterium tuberculosis — tuberculous osteomyelitis Etiology / Causes 1. 2. 3. Hematogenous spread Microorganisms reach bone through the bloodstream. Common in: ◦ ◦ ◦ ◦ ◦ Children Metaphysis of long bones Femur Tibia Humerus Direct implantation Organisms are introduced directly into bone following: ◦ ◦ ◦ ◦ ◦ Open fractures Penetrating injuries Orthopaedic surgery Internal fixation Joint replacement Contiguous spread Infection spreads from adjacent soft tissue. Examples: ◦ ◦ Diabetic foot ulcer Pressure sore Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 1 of 23◦ ◦ Cellulitis Chronic wound Predisposing factors • • • • • • • • • • Diabetes mellitus Immunosuppression Malnutrition Peripheral vascular disease Trauma Open fracture Previous surgery Prosthetic implants Intravenous drug use Chronic skin infection Pathophysiology Infection → bacterial multiplication → inflammatory response → vascular congestion → edema → increased intraosseous pressure → vascular compromise → bone necrosis → abscess formation → periosteal elevation → sequestrum formation → involucrum formation → sinus formation Important terms: Sequestrum: Dead, separated piece of infected bone. Involucrum: New bone formed around a sequestrum by the periosteum. Cloaca: Opening in the involucrum through which pus or sequestrum may discharge. Clinical Features Acute osteomyelitis Local: • • • • • • • Severe localized bone pain Tenderness Swelling Warmth Redness Restricted movement of nearby joint Muscle spasm Systemic: • • • • • • Fever Chills Malaise Irritability Tachycardia Loss of appetite Chronic osteomyelitis • • • Persistent bone pain Recurrent swelling Discharging sinus Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 2 of 23• • • • • • • • Purulent discharge Recurrent fever Non-healing wound Limb deformity Reduced function Local tenderness Skin pigmentation/scarring Sequestrum may occasionally be palpable Investigations Blood investigations • • • • • CBC — leukocytosis in acute infection ESR — raised CRP — raised Blood culture — identifies causative organism Blood glucose Imaging • • • • • X-ray Ultrasound for soft-tissue collection MRI — highly sensitive for early osteomyelitis CT — useful for cortical destruction and sequestrum Bone scan where indicated Microbiological investigation • • Pus/wound culture Bone biopsy and culture — important in chronic or uncertain cases Complications Acute • • • • • • • Septicemia Septic shock Subperiosteal abscess Soft-tissue abscess Pathological fracture Growth disturbance in children Joint infection Chronic • • • • • • • • • Recurrent sinus Sequestrum Involucrum Pathological fracture Bone deformity Limb shortening Secondary amyloidosis Malignant transformation of chronic sinus — classically squamous cell carcinoma (Marjolin ulcer) Chronic pain Page 3 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersFunctional disability • Medical Management Acute osteomyelitis • • • • • • • • • Hospitalization when clinically indicated Blood and tissue cultures Appropriate intravenous antibiotics initially Antibiotics modified according to culture and sensitivity Analgesics Antipyretics Adequate hydration and nutrition Immobilization of affected limb if necessary Treatment of underlying disease Chronic osteomyelitis • • • • • • • Long-term culture-directed antibiotics Wound care Nutritional support Diabetes control Treatment of vascular insufficiency Analgesia Physiotherapy after acute infection is controlled Surgical Management Indications: • • • • • • • Abscess Sequestrum Failure of antibiotics Chronic sinus Necrotic tissue Infected implant Pathological fracture Procedures: • • • • • • • • • • • Incision and drainage Surgical debridement Sequestrectomy Curettage Saucerization Removal of infected implants when appropriate Dead-space management Bone grafting Local antibiotic delivery Soft-tissue flap coverage Stabilization of pathological fracture when required Page 4 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersFlow Chart Infection of bone ↓ Inflammatory response ↓ Edema + increased intraosseous pressure ↓ Vascular compromise ↓ Bone necrosis ↓ Abscess / sequestrum ↓ Chronic infection ↓ Involucrum + sinus formation ↓ Debridement + antibiotics ± reconstruction Conclusion: Osteomyelitis is a potentially destructive bone infection. Early diagnosis, appropriate culture-directed antibiotics and timely drainage/debridement are essential to prevent chronic infection, deformity and functional disability. 2. BRODIE’S ABSCESS Definition Brodie’s abscess is a localized form of subacute osteomyelitis characterized by a localized intraosseous abscess, usually occurring in the metaphysis of a long bone, especially the tibia. The common causative organism is Staphylococcus aureus. Etiology Usually caused by: • Staphylococcus aureus Predisposing factors: • • • • • Previous minor trauma Hematogenous infection Reduced immunity Diabetes Previous inadequately treated infection Common Sites • • • • Upper end of tibia — common Lower end of femur Other long bones Occasionally small bones Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 5 of 23Pathophysiology Bacterial infection ↓ Localized inflammatory response ↓ Intraosseous abscess formation ↓ Granulation tissue around abscess ↓ Reactive sclerosis of surrounding bone Because the infection is localized and relatively slow, systemic manifestations may be minimal. Clinical Features • • • • • • • • Localized deep bone pain Pain may be worse at night Local tenderness Mild swelling Symptoms may persist for weeks or months Fever usually absent or mild Restriction of nearby joint movement may occur Symptoms may be relieved temporarily by salicylates/analgesics Investigations Blood • • • CBC may be normal ESR may be mildly elevated CRP may be elevated X-ray Typical appearance: • • • Well-defined radiolucent cavity Surrounding sclerosis Usually metaphyseal location MRI Shows: • • • CT Abscess cavity Bone marrow edema Soft-tissue involvement Useful for: • • Defining cortical involvement Surgical planning Page 6 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersBiopsy May be required to: • • • Confirm diagnosis Exclude tumor Obtain culture Differential Diagnosis • • • • • • Osteosarcoma Ewing sarcoma Osteoid osteoma Chronic osteomyelitis Bone cyst Tuberculosis of bone Complications • • • • • • Chronic osteomyelitis Recurrence Extension into soft tissues Pathological fracture — uncommon Growth disturbance in children Deformity Medical Management • • • • • Culture-directed antibiotics Analgesics Rest/temporary immobilization if required Treatment of predisposing conditions Follow-up imaging Surgical Management Surgery may be required when: • • • • • Abscess is large Symptoms persist despite antibiotics Diagnosis is uncertain There is extensive bone destruction Recurrence occurs Procedures: • • • • • Curettage of abscess cavity Drainage Removal of infected tissue Cavity filling with bone graft or suitable substitute when indicated Antibiotic therapy Page 7 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersFlow Chart Subacute bacterial infection ↓ Localized bone infection ↓ Intraosseous abscess ↓ Surrounding sclerosis ↓ Persistent localized pain ↓ X-ray/MRI diagnosis ↓ Antibiotics ± curettage/drainage Conclusion: Brodie’s abscess should be suspected in patients with persistent localized bone pain and a well-defined lytic lesion surrounded by sclerosis. Early diagnosis prevents progression to chronic osteomyelitis. 3. TUBERCULOUS ARTHRITIS / TB OF SPINE AND MAJOR JOINTS Definition Tuberculosis of bone and joints is a chronic granulomatous infection caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, usually resulting from hematogenous spread from a primary focus, commonly the lungs. Tuberculosis may affect: • • • • • • • Spine — Pott’s disease Hip Knee Ankle Shoulder Elbow Wrist and other joints A. TUBERCULOSIS OF SPINE — POTT’S SPINE Definition Pott’s spine is tuberculosis of the vertebral column, characterized by destruction of vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs, often leading to vertebral collapse, kyphotic deformity and neurological complications. Etiology Causative organism: • Mycobacterium tuberculosis Usually spreads to the spine through: • • • Hematogenous spread Lymphatic spread Extension from adjacent structures Common Sites • Lower thoracic spine Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 8 of 23• • Thoracolumbar junction Lumbar spine Pathophysiology TB infection ↓ Granuloma formation in vertebral body ↓ Caseous necrosis ↓ Vertebral destruction ↓ Disc involvement ↓ Vertebral collapse ↓ Kyphosis / gibbus deformity ↓ Cold abscess ± spinal cord compression ↓ Neurological deficit Clinical Features General: • • • • • Low-grade fever Night sweats Loss of appetite Weight loss Malaise Spinal: • • • • • • • • Persistent back pain Local tenderness Stiffness Muscle spasm Restricted spinal movement Deformity Kyphosis Gibbus deformity Neurological: • • • • • Weakness Sensory disturbance Radicular pain Paraplegia Bowel/bladder dysfunction in severe disease Cold abscess: May appear as: • Paravertebral swelling Page 9 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers• • Psoas abscess Groin swelling Investigations Blood: • • • • CBC ESR CRP Tuberculosis-related microbiological testing as appropriate Imaging: • • • X-ray spine MRI spine — best for neural compression, abscess and soft-tissue involvement CT — bony destruction and surgical planning Microbiological/histological: • • • Biopsy Tissue culture Molecular testing where appropriate Complications • • • • • • • • • • • Vertebral collapse Kyphosis Gibbus deformity Spinal cord compression Paraplegia Nerve-root compression Cold abscess Sinus formation Instability Chronic pain Severe neurological disability Medical Management Anti-tubercular therapy: Multidrug anti-tubercular therapy (ATT) is the cornerstone of treatment, using an appropriate regimen according to current national/institutional guidelines. Other measures: • • • • • • Analgesics Nutritional support Rest during active painful stage Bracing when appropriate Management of neurological deficits Monitoring treatment response Surgical Management Indications: • • • Progressive neurological deficit Significant spinal cord compression Severe instability Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 10 of 23• • • • Severe or progressive deformity Large abscess requiring drainage Failure of appropriate medical treatment Mechanical instability Procedures: • • • • • • Abscess drainage Debridement Decompression Stabilization Instrumented fusion Corrective surgery for severe deformity B. TUBERCULOUS ARTHRITIS OF MAJOR JOINTS Definition Tuberculous arthritis is a chronic granulomatous infection of a synovial joint caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, producing synovial inflammation, cartilage destruction and progressive joint damage. Common Joints • • • • • • Hip Knee Ankle Shoulder Elbow Wrist Clinical Features The onset is generally insidious. General: • • • • Low-grade fever Weight loss Malaise Reduced appetite Local: • • • • • • Pain Swelling Stiffness Reduced range of movement Muscle wasting Joint tenderness Characteristic: • A cold swelling may occur with comparatively little redness or warmth. Advanced disease: • • • • Sinus formation Joint destruction Deformity Subluxation/dislocation Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 11 of 23Ankylosis • • Limb shortening, especially with hip disease Investigations • • • • • • • • • • CBC ESR/CRP X-ray MRI Ultrasound for effusion Joint aspiration Synovial fluid analysis AFB testing/culture where appropriate Molecular tests Synovial biopsy Classical radiological findings: The classical description of tuberculous arthritis includes: • • • Periarticular osteoporosis Peripheral osseous erosions Gradual joint-space narrowing Complications • • • • • • • • • • Cartilage destruction Joint deformity Subluxation Dislocation Ankylosis Sinus formation Abscess Pathological fracture Limb shortening Severe functional disability Medical Management • • • • • • Appropriate multidrug ATT Analgesics Rest during acute painful stage Nutritional support Treatment of associated TB Physiotherapy after acute inflammation is controlled Physiotherapy principles: • • • • • Maintain safe joint mobility Prevent contractures Maintain muscle strength Gradual functional training Avoid excessive loading of a severely destroyed joint Page 12 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersSurgical Management Depending on disease stage: • • • • • • Aspiration/drainage of abscess Synovectomy in selected cases Debridement Arthrodesis for severely destroyed joints Joint replacement in selected patients after adequate infection control Spinal decompression and stabilization for Pott’s spine when indicated Conclusion: TB of the spine and major joints is a slowly progressive destructive disease. Early diagnosis and adequate ATT can prevent deformity and neurological or functional disability. 4. PYOGENIC ARTHRITIS Definition Pyogenic arthritis is an acute or chronic inflammatory infection of a synovial joint caused by pyogenic microorganisms, resulting in accumulation of infected synovial fluid/pus and potential destruction of articular cartilage. The terms pyogenic arthritis and septic arthritis are often used interchangeably. Etiology Common organisms: • • • • • Staphylococcus aureus — most common Streptococci Gram-negative bacilli Neisseria gonorrhoeae in appropriate populations Other organisms depending on age and risk factors Routes of Infection 1. 2. 3. Hematogenous Most common route. Direct inoculation ◦ ◦ ◦ Penetrating injury Joint injection Surgery Contiguous spread ◦ ◦ ◦ Osteomyelitis Soft-tissue infection Bursitis Risk Factors • • • • • • • Diabetes Immunosuppression Older age Recent surgery Joint prosthesis IV drug use Skin infection Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 13 of 23• • Pre-existing joint disease Trauma Pathophysiology Clinical Features Acute presentation: • • • • • • • Severe joint pain Rapidly developing swelling Warmth Redness Tenderness Marked restriction of movement Severe pain on passive movement Systemic: • • • • Fever Chills Malaise Tachycardia Common joints: • • • • • Knee Hip Shoulder Elbow Ankle Investigations Bacterial entry into joint ↓ Synovial inflammation ↓ Neutrophil accumulation ↓ Purulent effusion ↓ Raised intra-articular pressure ↓ Reduced cartilage nutrition ↓ Cartilage destruction ↓ Subchondral bone destruction ↓ Joint deformity / ankylosis Blood: • • CBC ESR Page 14 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers• • CRP Blood cultures Joint aspiration — essential investigation: Synovial fluid may show: • • • • • Turbid/purulent appearance High leukocyte count Predominantly neutrophils Gram stain Culture and sensitivity Imaging: • • • X-ray Ultrasound MRI where required Complications • • • • • • • • • • Cartilage destruction Joint-space loss Osteomyelitis Joint deformity Ankylosis Sepsis Septic shock Pathological fracture Growth disturbance in children Permanent disability Medical Management This is an orthopedic emergency. • • • • • • • • Urgent joint aspiration Blood cultures Prompt empirical antibiotics after appropriate cultures, then modify according to results Analgesics Antipyretics Fluid management if systemically ill Treatment of underlying conditions Temporary immobilization in the acute painful stage Surgical Management Main objectives: • • • • Drain pus Reduce bacterial load Relieve intra-articular pressure Prevent cartilage destruction Procedures: • Needle aspiration Page 15 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers• • • • Arthroscopic lavage and drainage Open arthrotomy and drainage when indicated Debridement Repeat drainage if necessary After infection is controlled: • • • • Gradual mobilization ROM exercises Muscle strengthening Functional rehabilitation Flow Chart 5. SEPTIC ARTHRITIS Definition Bacterial invasion of joint ↓ Synovitis ↓ Purulent effusion ↓ Raised intra-articular pressure ↓ Cartilage destruction ↓ Joint destruction ↓ Early antibiotics + drainage ↓ Rehabilitation Conclusion: Pyogenic arthritis is a joint-threatening emergency. Early aspiration, appropriate antibiotics and adequate drainage are essential to preserve joint function. Septic arthritis is an infection of a joint caused by microorganisms, producing inflammation and destruction of the synovium, cartilage and sometimes subchondral bone. Important examination point: Pyogenic arthritis and septic arthritis are commonly used as synonyms. “Septic arthritis” is the broader clinical term, while “pyogenic arthritis” generally emphasizes bacterial suppurative infection. Etiology Infective organisms: • • • • • Staphylococcus aureus Streptococci Gram-negative organisms Gonococci Other organisms in immunocompromised patients Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 16 of 23Routes: • • • • • Hematogenous spread Direct inoculation Spread from adjacent osteomyelitis Surgical contamination Prosthetic joint infection Risk Factors • • • • • • • • • Diabetes mellitus Immunosuppression Rheumatoid arthritis Prosthetic joint Recent joint surgery Skin infection Intravenous drug use Extremes of age Previous septic arthritis Clinical Features Local: • • • • • • • Severe joint pain Swelling Warmth Redness Tenderness Restricted active and passive ROM Severe pain on passive movement Systemic: • • • • • Fever Chills Malaise Tachycardia Leukocytosis Hip septic arthritis: May present with: • • • • Severe hip/groin pain Refusal to bear weight Painful hip movement Flexion and external rotation posture in some cases Investigations Blood: • • • • CBC ESR CRP Blood cultures Page 17 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersJoint aspiration: Most important investigation: • • • • • • Appearance Cell count Differential count Gram stain Culture and sensitivity Crystal analysis where clinically appropriate Imaging: • • • X-ray Ultrasound MRI Complications • • • • • • • • • Articular cartilage destruction Osteomyelitis Joint deformity Fibrous/bony ankylosis Subluxation/dislocation Growth plate damage Sepsis Septic shock Permanent loss of joint function Medical Management • • • • • • • • Urgent orthopedic evaluation Joint aspiration Blood cultures Prompt IV antibiotics, then culture-directed therapy Analgesia Fluid/electrolyte management Treatment of underlying diseases Nutritional support Surgical Management Drainage methods: • • • Repeated needle aspiration in selected joints Arthroscopic drainage and lavage Open arthrotomy Indications for operative drainage: • • • • • • Large purulent collection Failure of aspiration Deep joint infection Hip infection Inadequate clinical response Loculated infection Page 18 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersAssociated abscess • Rehabilitation After infection is adequately controlled: Early: • • • • Pain control Gentle ROM Positioning Prevention of contractures Later: • • • • • • Active ROM Isometric exercises Progressive strengthening Weight-bearing progression Gait training Functional activities 6. SYPHILITIC INFECTION OF JOINTS Definition Syphilitic arthritis is a rare musculoskeletal manifestation of infection with Treponema pallidum, in which syphilis produces inflammatory or destructive changes involving joints, bones or surrounding tissues. It may occur in different stages of syphilis, particularly as a manifestation of late/congenital syphilis. Etiology Causative organism: • Treponema pallidum Transmission: • • Sexual transmission Congenital transmission from infected mother to fetus Types of Joint Involvement 1. 2. 3. 4. Acute syphilitic arthritis: May occur during secondary syphilis. Chronic syphilitic arthropathy: May occur in late disease. Charcot arthropathy: A neuropathic arthropathy associated with tabes dorsalis, a late neurological manifestation of syphilis. Congenital syphilitic skeletal disease: May affect: ◦ ◦ ◦ Long bones Joints Epiphyses/metaphyses Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam Answers Page 19 of 23Pathophysiology In late neurosyphilis: Clinical Features Acute syphilitic arthritis • • • • • • • Joint pain Joint swelling Stiffness Effusion Restricted movement May involve multiple joints Constitutional symptoms may occur Chronic syphilitic arthropathy • • • • Chronic joint pain or discomfort Swelling Progressive deformity Restricted movement Charcot joint Characteristic features: • • • Joint instability Treponema pallidum infection ↓ Systemic infection ↓ Inflammatory response ↓ Synovial and periarticular involvement ↓ Chronic tissue damage Dorsal column sensory loss ↓ Loss of proprioception and protective sensation ↓ Repetitive joint trauma ↓ Progressive neuropathic arthropathy ↓ Joint destruction and deformity Recurrent painless or relatively painless trauma Swelling • • • • Warmth Deformity Crepitus Subluxation/dislocation Page 20 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersSevere radiological destruction • The knee and hip can be affected, among other joints. Investigations Serological tests Non-treponemal tests: • • VDRL RPR Treponemal tests: • • TPHA/TPPA FTA-ABS or equivalent treponemal assays Joint investigations • • • Joint aspiration when indicated Synovial fluid analysis Culture to exclude pyogenic infection Imaging • • X-ray MRI when required Neurological evaluation If Charcot arthropathy is suspected: • • • • Sensory examination Proprioception Reflexes Neurological assessment Differential Diagnosis • • • • • • Rheumatoid arthritis Osteoarthritis Septic arthritis Tuberculous arthritis Gout Neuropathic arthropathy due to other causes Complications • • • • • • • • • Joint destruction Deformity Subluxation Dislocation Pathological fracture Instability Loss of function Secondary osteoarthritis Neurological disability in associated neurosyphilis Page 21 of 23 Diseases of Bones and Joints — 20-Mark University Exam AnswersMedical Management Specific treatment Penicillin-based therapy is the standard treatment for syphilis, with the exact preparation, dose and duration determined by the stage and clinical form of disease. Management may include: • • • • • • Appropriate penicillin therapy Treatment of sexual partners according to public-health guidance Screening for other sexually transmitted infections Neurological evaluation when indicated Analgesics Management of secondary complications For Charcot arthropathy • • • • • Protection from excessive loading Off-loading Appropriate orthosis Footwear modification where applicable Management of associated neurological disease Surgical Management Surgery is generally directed toward the resulting structural damage rather than eradication of the infection itself. May include: • • • • • • Synovectomy in selected cases Debridement when indicated Arthrodesis for severe instability Joint reconstruction Joint replacement in selected patients after appropriate infection treatment Correction of severe deformity Rehabilitation • • • • • • • • Protection of affected joint Pain management Maintaining safe ROM Muscle strengthening Orthotic support Gait training Assistive devices when required Patient education regarding joint protection\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:36:44+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":17,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:06:44Z","content":"--- **1 OSTEOMYELITIS ACUTE AND CHRONIC** **Definition** Osteomyelitis is an inflammatory and infective disease of bone and bone marrow usually caused by microorganisms most commonly bacteria resulting in destruction of bone and surrounding tissues Acute osteomyelitis Infection of bone of relatively recent onset usually associated with acute inflammation and systemic manifestations Chronic osteomyelitis Persistent or recurrent bone infection lasting for a prolonged period usually associated with necrotic bone sequestrum new bone formation involucrum sinus formation and recurrent discharge **Common Causative Organism** Staphylococcus aureus is the most common organism Other organisms • Streptococci • Gram negative bacilli • Pseudomonas aeruginosa • Salmonella particularly in sickle cell disease • Mycobacterium tuberculosis tuberculous osteomyelitis **Etiology / Causes** 1 Hematogenous spread Microorganisms reach bone through the bloodstream Common in ◦ Children ◦ Metaphysis of long bones ◦ Femur ◦ Tibia ◦ Humerus 2 Direct implantation Organisms are introduced directly into bone following ◦ Open fractures ◦ Penetrating injuries ◦ Orthopaedic surgery ◦ Internal fixation ◦ Joint replacement 3 Contiguous spread Infection spreads from adjacent soft tissue Examples ◦ Diabetic foot ulcer ◦ Pressure sore ◦ Cellulitis ◦ Chronic wound **Predisposing factors** • Diabetes mellitus • Immunosuppression • Malnutrition • Peripheral vascular disease • Trauma • Open fracture • Previous surgery • Prosthetic implants • Intravenous drug use • Chronic skin infection **Pathophysiology** Infection → bacterial multiplication → inflammatory response → vascular congestion → edema → increased intraosseous pressure → vascular compromise → bone necrosis → abscess formation → periosteal elevation → sequestrum formation → involucrum formation → sinus formation Important terms Sequestrum Dead separated piece of infected bone Involucrum New bone formed around a sequestrum by the periosteum Cloaca Opening in the involucrum through which pus or sequestrum may discharge **Clinical Features** **Acute osteomyelitis** Local • Severe localized bone pain • Tenderness • Swelling • Warmth • Redness • Restricted movement of nearby joint • Muscle spasm Systemic • Fever • Chills • Malaise • Irritability • Tachycardia • Loss of appetite **Chronic osteomyelitis** • Persistent bone pain • Recurrent swelling • Discharging sinus • Purulent discharge • Recurrent fever • Non healing wound • Limb deformity • Reduced function • Local tenderness • Skin pigmentation/scarring • Sequestrum may occasionally be palpable **Investigations** **Blood investigations** • CBC leukocytosis in acute infection • ESR raised • CRP raised • Blood culture identifies causative organism • Blood glucose **Imaging** • X ray • Ultrasound for soft tissue collection • MRI highly sensitive for early osteomyelitis • CT useful for cortical destruction and sequestrum • Bone scan where indicated **Microbiological investigation** • Pus/wound culture • Bone biopsy and culture important in chronic or uncertain cases **Complications** **Acute** • Septicemia • Septic shock • Subperiosteal abscess • Soft tissue abscess • Pathological fracture • Growth disturbance in children • Joint infection **Chronic** • Recurrent sinus • Sequestrum • Involucrum • Pathological fracture • Bone deformity • Limb shortening • Secondary amyloidosis • Malignant transformation of chronic sinus classically squamous cell carcinoma Marjolin ulcer • Chronic pain • Functional disability **Medical Management** **Acute osteomyelitis** • Hospitalization when clinically indicated • Blood and tissue cultures • Appropriate intravenous antibiotics initially • Antibiotics modified according to culture and sensitivity • Analgesics • Antipyretics • Adequate hydration and nutrition • Immobilization of affected limb if necessary • Treatment of underlying disease **Chronic osteomyelitis** • Long term culture directed antibiotics • Wound care • Nutritional support • Diabetes control • Treatment of vascular insufficiency • Analgesia • Physiotherapy after acute infection is controlled **Surgical Management** Indications • Abscess • Sequestrum • Failure of antibiotics • Chronic sinus • Necrotic tissue • Infected implant • Pathological fracture Procedures • Incision and drainage • Surgical debridement • Sequestrectomy • Curettage • Saucerization • Removal of infected implants when appropriate • Dead space management • Bone grafting • Local antibiotic delivery • Soft tissue flap coverage • Stabilization of pathological fracture when required **Flow Chart** Infection of bone ↓ Inflammatory response ↓ Edema + increased intraosseous pressure ↓ Vascular compromise ↓ Bone necrosis ↓ Abscess / sequestrum ↓ Chronic infection ↓ Involucrum + sinus formation ↓ Debridement + antibiotics ± reconstruction **Conclusion** Osteomyelitis is a potentially destructive bone infection Early diagnosis appropriate culture directed antibiotics and timely drainage/debridement are essential to prevent chronic infection deformity and functional disability --- **2 BRODIES ABSCESS** **Definition** Brodies abscess is a localized form of subacute osteomyelitis characterized by a localized intraosseous abscess usually occurring in the metaphysis of a long bone especially the tibia The common causative organism is Staphylococcus aureus **Etiology** Usually caused by • Staphylococcus aureus Predisposing factors • Previous minor trauma • Hematogenous infection • Reduced immunity • Diabetes • Previous inadequately treated infection **Common Sites** • Upper end of tibia common • Lower end of femur • Other long bones • Occasionally small bones **Pathophysiology** Bacterial infection ↓ Localized inflammatory response ↓ Intraosseous abscess formation ↓ Granulation tissue around abscess ↓ Reactive sclerosis of surrounding bone Because the infection is localized and relatively slow systemic manifestations may be minimal **Clinical Features** • Localized deep bone pain • Pain may be worse at night • Local tenderness • Mild swelling • Symptoms may persist for weeks or months • Fever usually absent or mild • Restriction of nearby joint movement may occur • Symptoms may be relieved temporarily by salicylates/analgesics **Investigations** **Blood** • CBC may be normal • ESR may be mildly elevated • CRP may be elevated **X ray** Typical appearance • Well defined radiolucent cavity • Surrounding sclerosis • Usually metaphyseal location **MRI** Shows • Abscess cavity • Bone marrow edema • Soft tissue involvement **CT** Useful for • Defining cortical involvement • Surgical planning **Biopsy** May be required to • Confirm diagnosis • Exclude tumor • Obtain culture **Differential Diagnosis** • Osteosarcoma • Ewing sarcoma • Osteoid osteoma • Chronic osteomyelitis • Bone cyst • Tuberculosis of bone **Complications** • Chronic osteomyelitis • Recurrence • Extension into soft tissues • Pathological fracture uncommon • Growth disturbance in children • Deformity **Medical Management** • Culture directed antibiotics • Analgesics • Rest/temporary immobilization if required • Treatment of predisposing conditions • Follow up imaging **Surgical Management** Surgery may be required when • Abscess is large • Symptoms persist despite antibiotics • Diagnosis is uncertain • There is extensive bone destruction • Recurrence occurs Procedures • Curettage of abscess cavity • Drainage • Removal of infected tissue • Cavity filling with bone graft or suitable substitute when indicated • Antibiotic therapy **Flow Chart** Subacute bacterial infection ↓ Localized bone infection ↓ Intraosseous abscess ↓ Surrounding sclerosis ↓ Persistent localized pain ↓ X ray/MRI diagnosis ↓ Antibiotics ± curettage/drainage **Conclusion** Brodies abscess should be suspected in patients with persistent localized bone pain and a well defined lytic lesion surrounded by sclerosis Early diagnosis prevents progression to chronic osteomyelitis --- **3 TUBERCULOUS ARTHRITIS / TB OF SPINE AND MAJOR JOINTS** **Definition** Tuberculosis of bone and joints is a chronic granulomatous infection caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis usually resulting from hematogenous spread from a primary focus commonly the lungs Tuberculosis may affect • Spine Potts disease • Hip • Knee • Ankle • Shoulder • Elbow • Wrist and other joints **A TUBERCULOSIS OF SPINE POTTS SPINE** **Definition** Potts spine is tuberculosis of the vertebral column characterized by destruction of vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs often leading to vertebral collapse kyphotic deformity and neurological complications **Etiology** Causative organism • Mycobacterium tuberculosis Usually spreads to the spine through • Hematogenous spread • Lymphatic spread • Extension from adjacent structures **Common Sites** • Lower thoracic spine • Thoracolumbar junction • Lumbar spine **Pathophysiology** TB infection ↓ Granuloma formation in vertebral body ↓ Caseous necrosis ↓ Vertebral destruction ↓ Disc involvement ↓ Vertebral collapse ↓ Kyphosis / gibbus deformity ↓ Cold abscess ± spinal cord compression ↓ Neurological deficit **Clinical Features** **General** • Low grade fever • Night sweats • Loss of appetite • Weight loss • Malaise **Spinal** • Persistent back pain • Local tenderness • Stiffness • Muscle spasm • Restricted spinal movement • Deformity • Kyphosis • Gibbus deformity **Neurological** • Weakness • Sensory disturbance • Radicular pain • Paraplegia • Bowel/bladder dysfunction in severe disease **Cold abscess** May appear as • Paravertebral swelling • Psoas abscess • Groin swelling **Investigations** **Blood** • CBC • ESR • CRP • Tuberculosis related microbiological testing as appropriate **Imaging** • X ray spine • MRI spine best for neural compression abscess and soft tissue involvement • CT bony destruction and surgical planning **Microbiological/histological** • Biopsy • Tissue culture • Molecular testing where appropriate **Complications** • Vertebral collapse • Kyphosis • Gibbus deformity • Spinal cord compression • Paraplegia • Nerve root compression • Cold abscess • Sinus formation • Instability • Chronic pain • Severe neurological disability **Medical Management** **Anti tubercular therapy** Multidrug anti tubercular therapy ATT is the cornerstone of treatment using an appropriate regimen according to current national/institutional guidelines **Other measures** • Analgesics • Nutritional support • Rest during active painful stage • Bracing when appropriate • Management of neurological deficits • Monitoring treatment response **Surgical Management** Indications • Progressive neurological deficit • Significant spinal cord compression • Severe instability • Severe or progressive deformity • Large abscess requiring drainage • Failure of appropriate medical treatment • Mechanical instability Procedures • Abscess drainage • Debridement • Decompression • Stabilization • Instrumented fusion • Corrective surgery for severe deformity **B TUBERCULOUS ARTHRITIS OF MAJOR JOINTS** **Definition** Tuberculous arthritis is a chronic granulomatous infection of a synovial joint caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis producing synovial inflammation cartilage destruction and progressive joint damage **Common Joints** • Hip • Knee • Ankle • Shoulder • Elbow • Wrist **Clinical Features** The onset is generally insidious **General** • Low grade fever • Weight loss • Malaise • Reduced appetite **Local** • Pain • Swelling • Stiffness • Reduced range of movement • Muscle wasting • Joint tenderness **Characteristic** • A cold swelling may occur with comparatively little redness or warmth **Advanced disease** • Sinus formation • Joint destruction • Deformity • Subluxation/dislocation • Ankylosis • Limb shortening especially with hip disease **Investigations** • CBC • ESR/CRP • X ray • MRI • Ultrasound for effusion • Joint aspiration • Synovial fluid analysis • AFB testing/culture where appropriate • Molecular tests • Synovial biopsy **Classical radiological findings** The classical description of tuberculous arthritis includes • Periarticular osteoporosis • Peripheral osseous erosions • Gradual joint space narrowing **Complications** • Cartilage destruction • Joint deformity • Subluxation • Dislocation • Ankylosis • Sinus formation • Abscess • Pathological fracture • Limb shortening • Severe functional disability **Medical Management** • Appropriate multidrug ATT • Analgesics • Rest during acute painful stage • Nutritional support • Treatment of associated TB • Physiotherapy after acute inflammation is controlled **Physiotherapy principles** • Maintain safe joint mobility • Prevent contractures • Maintain muscle strength • Gradual functional training • Avoid excessive loading of a severely destroyed joint **Surgical Management** Depending on disease stage • Aspiration/drainage of abscess • Synovectomy in selected cases • Debridement • Arthrodesis for severely destroyed joints • Joint replacement in selected patients after adequate infection control • Spinal decompression and stabilization for Potts spine when indicated **Conclusion** TB of the spine and major joints is a slowly progressive destructive disease Early diagnosis and adequate ATT can prevent deformity and neurological or functional disability --- **4 PYOGENIC ARTHRITIS** **Definition** Pyogenic arthritis is an acute or chronic inflammatory infection of a synovial joint caused by pyogenic microorganisms resulting in accumulation of infected synovial fluid/pus and potential destruction of articular cartilage The terms pyogenic arthritis and septic arthritis are often used interchangeably **Etiology** Common organisms • Staphylococcus aureus most common • Streptococci • Gram negative bacilli • Neisseria gonorrhoeae in appropriate populations • Other organisms depending on age and risk factors **Routes of Infection** 1 Hematogenous Most common route 2 Direct inoculation ◦ Penetrating injury ◦ Joint injection ◦ Surgery 3 Contiguous spread ◦ Osteomyelitis ◦ Soft tissue infection ◦ Bursitis **Risk Factors** • Diabetes • Immunosuppression • Older age • Recent surgery • Joint prosthesis • IV drug use • Skin infection • Pre existing joint disease • Trauma **Pathophysiology** Bacterial entry into joint ↓ Synovial inflammation ↓ Neutrophil accumulation ↓ Purulent effusion ↓ Raised intra articular pressure ↓ Reduced cartilage nutrition ↓ Cartilage destruction ↓ Subchondral bone destruction ↓ Joint deformity / ankylosis **Clinical Features** **Acute presentation** • Severe joint pain • Rapidly developing swelling • Warmth • Redness • Tenderness • Marked restriction of movement • Severe pain on passive movement **Systemic** • Fever • Chills • Malaise • Tachycardia **Common joints** • Knee • Hip • Shoulder • Elbow • Ankle **Investigations** **Blood** • CBC • ESR • CRP • Blood cultures **Joint aspiration essential investigation** Synovial fluid may show • Turbid/purulent appearance • High leukocyte count • Predominantly neutrophils • Gram stain • Culture and sensitivity **Imaging** • X ray • Ultrasound • MRI where required **Complications** • Cartilage destruction • Joint space loss • Osteomyelitis • Joint deformity • Ankylosis • Sepsis • Septic shock • Pathological fracture • Growth disturbance in children • Permanent disability **Medical Management** This is an orthopedic emergency • Urgent joint aspiration • Blood cultures • Prompt empirical antibiotics after appropriate cultures then modify according to results • Analgesics • Antipyretics • Fluid management if systemically ill • Treatment of underlying conditions • Temporary immobilization in the acute painful stage **Surgical Management** Main objectives • Drain pus • Reduce bacterial load • Relieve intra articular pressure • Prevent cartilage destruction Procedures • Needle aspiration • Arthroscopic lavage and drainage • Open arthrotomy and drainage when indicated • Debridement • Repeat drainage if necessary **After infection is controlled** • Gradual mobilization • ROM exercises • Muscle strengthening • Functional rehabilitation **Flow Chart** Bacterial invasion of joint ↓ Synovitis ↓ Purulent effusion ↓ Raised intra articular pressure ↓ Cartilage destruction ↓ Joint destruction ↓ Early antibiotics + drainage ↓ Rehabilitation **Conclusion** Pyogenic arthritis is a joint threatening emergency Early aspiration appropriate antibiotics and adequate drainage are essential to preserve joint function --- **5 SEPTIC ARTHRITIS** **Definition** Septic arthritis is an infection of a joint caused by microorganisms producing inflammation and destruction of the synovium cartilage and sometimes subchondral bone Important examination point Pyogenic arthritis and septic arthritis are commonly used as synonyms Septic arthritis is the broader clinical term while pyogenic arthritis generally emphasizes bacterial suppurative infection **Etiology** Infective organisms • Staphylococcus aureus • Streptococci • Gram negative organisms • Gonococci • Other organisms in immunocompromised patients Routes • Hematogenous spread • Direct inoculation • Spread from adjacent osteomyelitis • Surgical contamination • Prosthetic joint infection **Risk Factors** • Diabetes mellitus • Immunosuppression • Rheumatoid arthritis • Prosthetic joint • Recent joint surgery • Skin infection • Intravenous drug use • Extremes of age • Previous septic arthritis **Clinical Features** **Local** • Severe joint pain • Swelling • Warmth • Redness • Tenderness • Restricted active and passive ROM • Severe pain on passive movement **Systemic** • Fever • Chills • Malaise • Tachycardia • Leukocytosis **Hip septic arthritis** May present with • Severe hip/groin pain • Refusal to bear weight • Painful hip movement • Flexion and external rotation posture in some cases **Investigations** **Blood** • CBC • ESR • CRP • Blood cultures **Joint aspiration Most important investigation** • Appearance • Cell count • Differential count • Gram stain • Culture and sensitivity • Crystal analysis where clinically appropriate **Imaging** • X ray • Ultrasound • MRI **Complications** • Articular cartilage destruction • Osteomyelitis • Joint deformity • Fibrous/bony ankylosis • Subluxation/dislocation • Growth plate damage • Sepsis • Septic shock • Permanent loss of joint function **Medical Management** • Urgent orthopedic evaluation • Joint aspiration • Blood cultures • Prompt IV antibiotics then culture directed therapy • Analgesia • Fluid/electrolyte management • Treatment of underlying diseases • Nutritional support **Surgical Management** Drainage methods • Repeated needle aspiration in selected joints • Arthroscopic drainage and lavage • Open arthrotomy Indications for operative drainage • Large purulent collection • Failure of aspiration • Deep joint infection • Hip infection • Inadequate clinical response • Loculated infection • Associated abscess **Rehabilitation** After infection is adequately controlled **Early** • Pain control • Gentle ROM • Positioning • Prevention of contractures **Later** • Active ROM • Isometric exercises • Progressive strengthening • Weight bearing progression • Gait training • Functional activities --- **6 SYPHILITIC INFECTION OF JOINTS** **Definition** Syphilitic arthritis is a rare musculoskeletal manifestation of infection with Treponema pallidum in which syphilis produces inflammatory or destructive changes involving joints bones or surrounding tissues It may occur in different stages of syphilis particularly as a manifestation of late/congenital syphilis **Etiology** Causative organism • Treponema pallidum Transmission • Sexual transmission • Congenital transmission from infected mother to fetus **Types of Joint Involvement** 1 Acute syphilitic arthritis May occur during secondary syphilis 2 Chronic syphilitic arthropathy May occur in late disease 3 Charcot arthropathy A neuropathic arthropathy associated with tabes dorsalis a late neurological manifestation of syphilis 4 Congenital syphilitic skeletal disease May affect ◦ Long bones ◦ Joints ◦ Epiphyses/metaphyses **Pathophysiology** Treponema pallidum infection ↓ Systemic infection ↓ Inflammatory response ↓ Synovial and periarticular involvement ↓ Chronic tissue damage In late neurosyphilis Dorsal column sensory loss ↓ Loss of proprioception and protective sensation ↓ Repetitive joint trauma ↓ Progressive neuropathic arthropathy ↓ Joint destruction and deformity **Clinical Features** **Acute syphilitic arthritis** • Joint pain • Joint swelling • Stiffness • Effusion • Restricted movement • May involve multiple joints • Constitutional symptoms may occur **Chronic syphilitic arthropathy** • Chronic joint pain or discomfort • Swelling • Progressive deformity • Restricted movement **Charcot joint** Characteristic features • Recurrent painless or relatively painless trauma • Swelling • Warmth • Deformity • Crepitus • Subluxation/dislocation • Severe radiological destruction • The knee and hip can be affected among other joints **Investigations** **Serological tests** Non treponemal tests • VDRL • RPR Treponemal tests • TPHA/TPPA • FTA ABS or equivalent treponemal assays **Joint investigations** • Joint aspiration when indicated • Synovial fluid analysis • Culture to exclude pyogenic infection **Imaging** • X ray • MRI when required **Neurological evaluation** If Charcot arthropathy is suspected • Sensory examination • Proprioception • Reflexes • Neurological assessment **Differential Diagnosis** • Rheumatoid arthritis • Osteoarthritis • Septic arthritis • Tuberculous arthritis • Gout • Neuropathic arthropathy due to other causes **Complications** • Joint destruction • Deformity • Subluxation • Dislocation • Pathological fracture • Instability • Loss of function • Secondary osteoarthritis • Neurological disability in associated neurosyphilis **Medical Management** **Specific treatment** Penicillin based therapy is the standard treatment for syphilis with the exact preparation dose and duration determined by the stage and clinical form of disease Management may include • Appropriate penicillin therapy • Treatment of sexual partners according to public health guidance • Screening for other sexually transmitted infections • Neurological evaluation when indicated • Analgesics • Management of secondary complications **For Charcot arthropathy** • Protection from excessive loading • Off loading • Appropriate orthosis • Footwear modification where applicable • Management of associated neurological disease **Surgical Management** Surgery is generally directed toward the resulting structural damage rather than eradication of the infection itself May include • Synovectomy in selected cases • Debridement when indicated • Arthrodesis for severe instability • Joint reconstruction • Joint replacement in selected patients after appropriate infection treatment • Correction of severe deformity **Rehabilitation** • Protection of affected joint • Pain management • Maintaining safe ROM • Muscle strengthening • Orthotic support • Gait training • Assistive devices when required • Patient education regarding joint protection","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool selection, always aiming for the most precise one available. Efficiency hinges on using specialized tools whenever possible, steering clear of more general-purpose options unless absolutely necessary. The goal is to minimize complexity and maximize effectiveness in every task.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now deeply considering the new guidelines. I'm actively thinking through how to eliminate extraneous content and establish a better information flow. My process starts by recalling critical instructions 1 and 2, which I find fundamental. Next, I am thinking specifically about the tools at my disposal, and their most effective application.\n\n\n**Clarifying Textual Formatting**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the specific formatting requirements. Maintaining the arrows and signs within flowcharts is confirmed, and I'm extending that to the whole text, keeping `-` as a minus sign. I am removing commas, periods, colons and brackets. I am also working on removing some specific artifacts.\n\n\n**Analyzing Text Patterns**\n\nI'm now identifying consistent textual elements. Recognizing multiple instances of \"Page X of 23\" and related structures is key. I'm noting the standard formatting for chapter headings, such as `1 OSTEOMYELITIS ACUTE AND CHRONIC` and considering best practices for parsing. I'm focusing on the conversion of hyphens to minus signs, while also considering how to represent \"Gram-negative\" effectively.\n\n\n**Examining Gram-Negative**\n\nI'm now carefully navigating how to represent \"Gram-negative\". I'm making sure it is not translated to minus signs, while also attempting to remove punctuation from around the term. I'm also now carefully examining the etiology section, and I'm focused on correctly identifying all types of spreading and predisposing factors to highlight them. I am paying close attention to keeping the bullet points.\n\n\n**Defining Infection Stages**\n\nI'm now outlining infection development, from bacterial multiplication to sinus formation. Key terms like \"sequestrum\" and \"involucrum\" are pivotal here. I'm distinguishing acute versus chronic osteomyelitis, identifying their specific local and systemic clinical features, and noting how these symptoms progress over time.\n\n\n**Detailing Clinical Features**\n\nI am now focusing on clinical features, and I'm detailing the key aspects of both acute and chronic osteomyelitis. I am classifying complications, specifically septicemia, septic shock, and pathological fracture, and separating these by acute versus chronic presentation. I am also identifying critical diagnostic tools like X-ray, Ultrasound, and MRI for accurate assessment.\n\n\n**Outlining Surgical Options**\n\nI'm now carefully mapping out surgical approaches. I've broken down the procedures into a few key areas, and I am focused on the specifics. I'm focusing on the reasons to do surgery, as well as the flow of infection to better grasp the appropriate surgical response. I'm making sure to categorize the procedures effectively.\n\n\n**Summarizing Procedures**\n\nI'm now summarizing the key aspects of surgical approaches and their corresponding procedures. I'm focusing on the infection flow and reasoning for interventions. I'm prioritizing the steps, and focusing on the relationship between debridement, antibiotic use, and potential reconstruction, with a particular focus on chronic infection.\n\n\n**Describing Localized Infection**\n\nI'm now describing the localized infection and its clinical presentation. I'm focusing on the inflammatory response, abscess formation, and surrounding sclerosis. I'm noting the minimal systemic manifestations due to the localized nature of the infection. I'm also carefully documenting clinical features such as pain, tenderness, swelling, and fever.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Findings**\n\nI'm now integrating information on complications such as chronic osteomyelitis, recurrence, and growth disturbances, into a single, cohesive framework. I'm noting that the treatment includes both medical (antibiotics, analgesics) and surgical strategies (curettage, drainage). I'm also now structuring the flowchart and conclusion based on this current research.\n\n\n**Defining TB Spinal Spread**\n\nI am now focusing on tuberculous arthritis, specifically of the spine. I'm noting the progression from hematogenous spread to localized spinal infections, and the key clinical features of Pott's disease. I'm zeroing in on defining the initial spread, with particular attention to how the lower thoracic spine is affected.\n\n\n**Mapping Disease Manifestations**\n\nI'm now detailing the full scope of TB spinal spread and its clinical presentation. I'm carefully organizing the process, starting from the infection's entrance to the eventual neurological effects. I'm focusing on the formation of granulomas, caseous necrosis, vertebral destruction, and spinal deformities like kyphosis. I'm also capturing the related clinical features, from fever and weight loss, to back pain and neurological deficits.\n\n\n**Outlining Surgical Interventions**\n\nI'm now outlining the surgical interventions based on clinical indications, specifically: neurological deficit, spinal compression, and severe instability. I'm noting the need for abscess drainage, and the importance of medical management. Also, I'm integrating microbiological and histological testing for accurate diagnosis and tailored treatments.\n\n\n**Analyzing Joint Infection**\n\nI'm now analyzing tuberculous arthritis in major joints, considering the infection's definition and common locations. I'm focusing on clinical features, from general symptoms like fever and weight loss, to local signs such as pain, swelling, and reduced movement, noting how the disease progresses. Also, I'm documenting advanced manifestations, including sinus formation and joint destruction.\n\n\n**Evaluating Diagnostic Tools**\n\nI'm now focusing on diagnosing tuberculous arthritis. I'm prioritizing radiological findings, specifically periarticular osteoporosis, peripheral osseous erosions, and joint space narrowing. I'm also carefully documenting all the complications and detailing the medical management process, from ATT to physiotherapy principles.\n\n\n**Defining Treatment Protocols**\n\nI'm now outlining treatment protocols for pyogenic arthritis, noting that interventions depend on the disease stage. I'm focusing on key procedures like aspiration, drainage, debridement, synovectomy, and arthrodesis. I'm also planning to integrate this information into the flowchart, with the final goal of providing a comprehensive guide for managing this complex condition.\n\n\n**Describing Joint Infection**\n\nI'm now describing joint infection's diverse routes of entry, categorizing them as hematogenous, direct inoculation, and contiguous spread. I'm noting the significance of the risk factors: diabetes, immunosuppression, and older age. My focus is on the disease's natural progression from bacterial entry to potential ankylosis. I'm also capturing clinical features such as severe joint pain, swelling, warmth, and systemic symptoms, including fever and chills.\n\n\n**Analyzing Joint Complications**\n\nI'm now analyzing the complications of joint infections, including cartilage destruction and sepsis. I'm noting the vital role of investigations such as blood tests and imaging. I'm focusing on key clinical features, imaging like X-ray, Ultrasound, and MRI for accurate assessment. Now, I'm carefully organizing both medical and surgical management, focusing on key aspects like aspiration and antibiotic use. I am also documenting how this informs future planning.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Treatment Steps**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on treatment steps. I'm focusing on the necessity of addressing intra-articular pressure and preventing cartilage damage. I'm making sure to describe key procedures like aspiration, arthroscopic drainage, and open arthrotomy, as well as debridement, and rehabilitation. I'm also capturing the infection progression, to ensure the steps are correctly ordered. I'm also now analyzing the joint threat of Pyogenic arthritis.\n\n\n**Defining Arthritis Features**\n\nI am now differentiating between septic and pyogenic arthritis. I'm noting that the latter specifically indicates a bacterial suppurative infection. Then, I'm carefully reviewing the key etiologic agents like Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococci, and Gram-negative organisms. I'm also now capturing specific routes of infection, including hematogenous spread, and other important factors like surgical contamination. I'm making sure to define and capture all the risk factors, including diabetes and immunosuppression. Lastly, I'm documenting all of the clinical features.\n\n\n**Detailing Treatments**\n\nI'm now detailing all treatments for joint infections. I'm noting investigations like blood tests and imaging are critical, and especially emphasizing joint aspiration as the most important. I'm also capturing clinical features and complications of infection. I'm organizing medical management, and including intravenous antibiotics and pain relief. Finally, I'm documenting all surgical drainage methods, including open arthrotomy.\n\n\n**Outlining Rehabilitation Plans**\n\nI'm now detailing rehabilitation following infection control, highlighting early and later stages. Early rehabilitation will focus on pain control, ROM, positioning, and contracture prevention. Later, I will integrate active ROM, isometric exercises, progressive strengthening, gait training, and functional activities to ensure a full recovery. I'm noting syphilitic infection of joints for later integration.\n\n\n**Describing Syphilitic Arthritis**\n\nI'm now detailing congenital syphilitic skeletal disease, focusing on long bones, joints, and epiphyses. I'm noting the pathophysiology, from Treponema pallidum to chronic tissue damage. I'm also capturing clinical features of acute and chronic syphilitic arthritis, including Charcot joint characteristics such as trauma, deformity and radiological destruction.\n\n\n**Analyzing Diagnostic Tools**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on diagnosing syphilitic arthritis. Serological tests, like VDRL and TPHA/TPPA, are key, alongside joint aspirations. I'm noting X-rays and MRI are essential for imaging, and neurological evaluations for Charcot arthropathy. I am also making sure to list all the differential diagnoses, including rheumatoid arthritis and septic arthritis.\n\n\n**Integrating Treatment Details**\n\nI am now integrating detailed treatment approaches. I'm focusing on penicillin therapy, partner treatment, and STD screening. I'm including neurological evaluations and analgesics. I'm also capturing Charcot arthropathy's management, including off-loading and appropriate orthoses, and the necessary footwear modifications. I'm including the surgical and rehab approach.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":18,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:11:45Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nRULE 1 Remove these type of lines you have inside your text for each of every pages, because I have copied them  ----- Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 1 of 17                                                        RULE 2 There are flowcharts in which arrows are present and signs are present of plus minus. You don't have to remove them. You can just give in continuous format as it is. Don't remove them  Here is the text. ------------------           1. OSTEOMA DEFINITION Osteoma is a benign, slow-growing tumour composed of mature compact or cancellous bone. It commonly occurs in the skull and facial bones and rarely causes malignant transformation. CLASSIFICATION According to site Central osteoma: Develops within bone. Peripheral osteoma: Develops from the surface of bone. According to structure Compact osteoma Cancellous osteoma ETIOLOGY The exact cause is uncertain. Possible factors: Developmental abnormality Genetic factors Previous trauma Chronic irritation Associated syndromes, particularly Gardner syndrome CLINICAL FEATURES Small osteomas may be asymptomatic. When symptomatic: Slow-growing hard swelling Painless mass Facial asymmetry Headache in some cranial lesions Sinus obstruction Visual symptoms if orbital structures are involved Cosmetic deformity • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 1 of 17INVESTIGATIONS • • • • X-ray: Dense, well-defined bony lesion CT scan: Best for defining the size, location and relationship to surrounding structures. MRI: Useful when soft-tissue or intracranial involvement is suspected. Biopsy: Usually not required for a typical small osteoma but may be performed when diagnosis is uncertain. COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • Cosmetic deformity Pressure on adjacent structures Sinus obstruction Visual impairment Rare neurological complications Recurrence after incomplete excision MEDICAL MANAGEMENT • • Asymptomatic: Observation, Periodic clinical/imaging follow-up Symptomatic: Analgesics for pain, Treatment of associated sinus symptoms SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Indications: Pain, Functional impairment, Significant cosmetic deformity, Rapid or uncertain growth, Compression of adjacent structures. Procedure: Complete surgical excision is the treatment of choice for symptomatic lesions. PROGNOSIS Osteoma is benign and usually has an excellent prognosis. Recurrence is uncommon after complete excision. 2. OSTEOSARCOMA DEFINITION Osteosarcoma is a malignant primary bone tumour in which the malignant tumour cells produce osteoid or immature bone. It is the most common primary malignant bone tumour in many clinical series, particularly among adolescents. CLASSIFICATION 1. Conventional high-grade osteosarcoma • • • Osteoblastic Chondroblastic Fibroblastic 2. Other types • • • • Telangiectatic osteosarcoma Parosteal osteosarcoma Periosteal osteosarcoma Secondary osteosarcoma COMMON SITES Usually occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Common sites: • • • Lower end of femur Upper end of tibia Upper end of humerus Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 2 of 17ETIOLOGY / RISK FACTORS Exact cause is unknown. Risk factors: • • • • • • Previous radiotherapy Paget disease of bone Hereditary retinoblastoma Li-Fraumeni syndrome Certain genetic abnormalities Rapid skeletal growth may be associated with adolescent presentation PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Malignant transformation of osteogenic cells ↓ Rapid tumour growth ↓ Production of malignant osteoid ↓ Destruction of normal bone ↓ Extension into soft tissues ↓ Blood-borne metastasis (Most commonly lungs) CLINICAL FEATURES Local: • • • • Progressive bone pain (initially intermittent, later persistent) Swelling and tenderness Reduced movement of adjacent joint Limp if lower limb is affected Advanced: • • • • Large hard mass Venous congestion Pathological fracture Severe functional limitation Systemic: Systemic symptoms may be absent initially. INVESTIGATIONS • • • • • X-ray: May show mixed lytic and sclerotic lesion, cortical destruction, periosteal reaction, sunburst appearance, Codman’s triangle. MRI: Important for local tumour extent, neurovascular involvement, soft-tissue extension, surgical planning. CT chest: Used to assess pulmonary metastases. Bone scan / PET-CT: May be used for staging. Biopsy: Definitive diagnosis requires appropriate tumour biopsy, planned so that the biopsy tract can be removed during definitive surgery. COMPLICATIONS • • • • Pathological fracture Local tissue destruction Neurovascular involvement Pulmonary metastasis Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 3 of 17• • • Bone metastases Recurrence Death if untreated MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Osteosarcoma is usually treated using multimodal therapy. • • Chemotherapy: Usually given before surgery (neoadjuvant chemotherapy) and after surgery (adjuvant chemotherapy). Common agents may include combinations containing: High-dose methotrexate, Doxorubicin, Cisplatin. Supportive management: Analgesics, Nutritional support, Psychological support, Management of chemotherapy complications. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • Limb-salvage surgery: Preferred when adequate oncological margins can be achieved while preserving useful limb function. May involve wide excision, endoprosthetic reconstruction, structural bone grafting in selected cases. Amputation: Considered when limb salvage cannot achieve adequate tumour control, major neurovascular involvement prevents safe resection, or extensive local disease makes reconstruction unsuitable. PHYSIOTHERAPY • • Preoperative: Maintain ROM, Maintain muscle strength, Pain management, Psychological preparation. Postoperative: Wound and limb protection, Gradual ROM, Strengthening, Prosthetic training if amputation is performed, Gait training, Functional rehabilitation. PROGNOSIS Prognosis depends on: Tumour size, Site, Presence of metastasis, Response to chemotherapy, Surgical margins. 3. OSTEOCHONDROMA DEFINITION Osteochondroma is a benign bone-forming tumour characterized by a cartilage-capped bony projection that arises from the surface of bone and typically contains cortex and medulla continuous with the underlying bone. It is the most common benign bone tumour. TYPES • • Solitary osteochondroma Multiple hereditary osteochondromas (also called hereditary multiple exostoses) COMMON SITES Usually near the metaphysis of long bones: Distal femur, Proximal tibia, Proximal humerus. ETIOLOGY Usually developmental. Genetic mutations involving the EXT gene family are important in hereditary multiple osteochondromas. CLINICAL FEATURES Many are asymptomatic. Symptoms include: • • • • • • Painless bony swelling Cosmetic deformity Mechanical irritation Pain due to pressure on surrounding structures Restricted movement Limb-length discrepancy Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 4 of 17• • Nerve compression Vascular compression INVESTIGATIONS • • • X-ray: Characteristic bony projection from cortex, medullary continuity with parent bone. CT: Useful for complex anatomical areas. MRI: Useful to evaluate cartilage cap, neurovascular structures, suspicious malignant transformation. COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • • Mechanical irritation Fracture of stalk Nerve compression Vascular compression Deformity Limb-length discrepancy Rare malignant transformation into secondary chondrosarcoma MEDICAL MANAGEMENT • • Asymptomatic: Observation, Periodic review. Symptomatic: Analgesics, Activity modification, Physiotherapy for secondary functional problems. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Excision is indicated for: Pain, Functional impairment, Neurovascular compression, Significant deformity, Suspicion of malignant transformation, Growth after skeletal maturity. Complete excision usually provides excellent results. 4. ENCHONDROMA DEFINITION Enchondroma is a benign cartilage-forming tumour that develops within the medullary cavity of bone. COMMON SITES Small bones of hands, Feet, Femur, Humerus, Tibia. ETIOLOGY Usually sporadic. Multiple enchondromas may occur in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome. CLINICAL FEATURES Often asymptomatic and discovered incidentally. Symptoms include: • • • • • Local swelling Pain Pathological fracture Deformity Reduced function Note: Pain in an otherwise uncomplicated lesion requires evaluation for other causes, including possible malignant transformation. INVESTIGATIONS • • • X-ray: May show well-defined intramedullary lucent lesion, scattered calcifications, cortical thinning, endosteal scalloping. MRI: Useful to assess cartilage characteristics, soft-tissue extension, aggressive features. CT: Useful for mineralization and cortical assessment. Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 5 of 17Biopsy: Required when imaging is atypical or malignancy is suspected. • COMPLICATIONS • • • • • Pathological fracture Deformity Recurrence Rare malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma Increased risk of malignancy in some multiple-lesion syndromes MANAGEMENT • • • Asymptomatic: Observation, Periodic clinical/imaging follow-up. Symptomatic: Curettage, Bone grafting or suitable cavity filling when indicated, Internal fixation if required for structural stability. Malignant transformation: Wide surgical excision according to tumour grade and location. 5. EWING’S SARCOMA DEFINITION Ewing sarcoma is a malignant small round cell tumour of bone or soft tissue, most commonly affecting children and adolescents. It is associated with characteristic genetic alterations, classically involving EWSR1, most often through an EWSR1-FLI1 fusion. COMMON SITES Pelvis, Femur, Tibia, Fibula, Ribs, Humerus, Vertebrae. ETIOLOGY The exact cause is unknown. Genetic translocations are characteristic. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Malignant cellular proliferation ↓ Bone marrow involvement ↓ Cortical destruction ↓ Periosteal reaction ↓ Soft-tissue extension ↓ Metastasis (Lungs, Bone, Bone marrow) CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • Bone pain, Swelling, Tenderness Fever, Malaise, Weight loss Reduced function, Limp Pathological fracture Because fever and inflammatory markers can be prominent, it may initially resemble infection. INVESTIGATIONS • • • Blood: CBC, ESR/CRP, LDH may have prognostic relevance. X-ray: Classically permeative/moth-eaten destruction, lamellated or “onion-skin” periosteal reaction. MRI: Defines local extent, soft-tissue involvement, neurovascular relationship. Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 6 of 17• • • CT chest: Pulmonary staging. PET-CT / Bone marrow assessment: May be used according to staging protocol. Biopsy: Required for definitive diagnosis. COMPLICATIONS • • • Pathological fracture, Local tissue destruction Pulmonary metastasis, Bone metastasis, Bone marrow involvement Recurrence, Treatment-related complications MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Ewing sarcoma is treated with systemic chemotherapy plus local control. Chemotherapy may include multi-agent regimens such as: Vincristine, Doxorubicin, Cyclophosphamide, Ifosfamide, Etoposide (exact regimen depends on contemporary oncology protocols). SURGICAL MANAGEMENT & RADIOTHERAPY • • Surgery: Wide local excision, Limb-salvage surgery, Reconstruction, Amputation in selected cases. Radiotherapy: Ewing sarcoma is relatively radiosensitive and radiotherapy may be used when surgery cannot achieve adequate margins, tumour is unresectable, local control is otherwise inadequate, or in selected high-risk situations. 6. GIANT CELL TUMOUR OF BONE DEFINITION Giant cell tumour (GCT) of bone is a generally benign but locally aggressive bone tumour composed of mononuclear stromal cells and numerous osteoclast-like giant cells. It can rarely metastasize, particularly to the lungs. AGE Usually occurs in young adults, most commonly between skeletal maturity and approximately 40 years. COMMON SITES Lower end of femur, Upper end of tibia, Distal radius, Proximal humerus. It characteristically involves the epiphysis extending to the subarticular bone. CLINICAL FEATURES • • • Pain, Progressive swelling, Tenderness Joint stiffness, Reduced joint movement Pathological fracture INVESTIGATIONS • • • • X-ray: Typical eccentric lytic lesion, expansile lesion, “soap-bubble” appearance, little or no matrix mineralization, extension to subchondral bone. MRI: Determines soft-tissue extension, relationship with neurovascular structures, surgical planning. Biopsy: Required for definitive diagnosis. Chest imaging: May be performed because rare pulmonary metastases can occur. COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • Pathological fracture Joint destruction Local recurrence Secondary osteoarthritis Rare pulmonary metastasis Rare malignant transformation Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 7 of 17MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Selected cases may be treated with Denosumab, particularly when surgery would cause unacceptable morbidity or in selected unresectable/recurrent disease. Pain management and supportive care. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • • Extended curettage: Often combined with high-speed burring, local adjuvants, bone graft/cement filling. Wide excision: Considered for extensive soft-tissue extension, recurrent disease, destructive lesions, selected aggressive tumours. Reconstruction: Bone graft, Cement, Prosthetic reconstruction, Arthroplasty where indicated. 7. MULTIPLE MYELOMA DEFINITION Multiple myeloma is a malignant plasma-cell disorder characterized by clonal proliferation of plasma cells in bone marrow, production of a monoclonal immunoglobulin or light chain, and characteristic bone destruction, anemia, renal dysfunction and hypercalcemia. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Clonal plasma-cell proliferation ↓ Bone marrow infiltration ↓ Osteoclast activation + osteoblast suppression ↓ Osteolytic bone lesions ↓ Hypercalcemia + pathological fractures COMMON SITES Vertebrae, Ribs, Skull, Pelvis, Proximal femur, Proximal humerus. CLINICAL FEATURES Mnemonic: CRAB • • • • C — HyperCalcemia: Nausea, Constipation, Confusion, Excessive thirst R — Renal impairment: Reduced renal function A — Anemia: Fatigue, Weakness, Pallor B — Bone lesions: Bone pain, Pathological fractures, Vertebral collapse, Reduced height Other features: Recurrent infections, Weight loss, Neurological symptoms due to vertebral collapse. INVESTIGATIONS • • • • Blood: CBC, Serum calcium, Renal function, Total protein, Serum protein electrophoresis, Immunofixation, Serum free light chains. Urine: Urine protein electrophoresis/immunofixation, Bence Jones protein/light chains. Bone marrow: Bone marrow aspiration/biopsy. Imaging: Low-dose whole-body CT, MRI, PET-CT where appropriate. COMPLICATIONS • • Pathological fractures, Vertebral collapse, Spinal cord compression Hypercalcemia, Renal failure, Severe anemia, Recurrent infections Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 8 of 17Amyloidosis, Thrombosis • MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Treatment is hematology/oncology directed. May include: • • • • • Combination anti-myeloma therapy (Proteasome inhibitors, Immunomodulatory drugs, Monoclonal antibodies, Corticosteroids) Bisphosphonates or denosumab in appropriate patients Radiotherapy for selected painful/local lesions Autologous stem-cell transplantation in eligible patients Supportive treatment: Hydration, Management of hypercalcemia, Renal support, Treatment of anemia, Infection prevention/treatment, Pain management. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Surgery is mainly for complications: Stabilization of pathological fractures, Vertebral augmentation in selected cases, Spinal decompression/stabilization for neurological compromise, Fixation of impending or established fractures. 8. METASTATIC BONE TUMOURS DEFINITION Metastatic bone tumour is a malignant tumour in bone that has spread from a primary malignant tumour located elsewhere in the body. It is more common than primary malignant bone tumours. COMMON PRIMARY TUMOURS Breast, Prostate, Lung, Kidney, Thyroid. TYPES • • • Osteolytic metastases: Cause bone destruction (Commonly: Renal cell carcinoma, Lung carcinoma, Thyroid carcinoma). Osteoblastic metastases: Cause increased bone formation/sclerosis (Classically: Prostate carcinoma). Mixed lesions: Breast and other cancers may produce mixed patterns. COMMON SITES Spine, Pelvis, Ribs, Proximal femur, Proximal humerus, Skull. CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • Persistent bone pain, Night pain, Back pain Pathological fracture, Reduced mobility Weight loss, Weakness Spinal metastasis: Radicular pain, Weakness, Sensory loss, Bowel/bladder dysfunction, Spinal cord compression. INVESTIGATIONS • • • Blood: CBC, Calcium, Alkaline phosphatase, Renal and liver function, Disease-specific tumour markers. Imaging: X-ray, CT, MRI, Bone scan, PET-CT. Biopsy: May be required if primary tumour is unknown, imaging is atypical, or histological confirmation is required before treatment. COMPLICATIONS • • Pathological fracture, Hypercalcemia, Spinal cord compression Severe pain, Paralysis, Reduced mobility, Hypercalcemic crisis, Death from primary malignancy Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 9 of 17MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Management depends on the primary cancer. May include: Systemic anticancer therapy, Hormonal therapy, Chemotherapy, Targeted therapy, Immunotherapy, Bisphosphonates, Denosumab, Analgesics. Radiotherapy: Particularly useful for painful metastases, local tumour control, spinal cord compression in selected circumstances. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Indications: Pathological fracture, Impending fracture, Spinal instability, Neurological compression, Severe mechanical pain. Procedures: Internal fixation, Intramedullary nailing, Plate fixation, Endoprosthetic reconstruction, Spinal decompression and stabilization. PART B — HIP AND AVASCULAR CONDITIONS 9. PERTHES DISEASE DEFINITION Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is a childhood disorder characterized by idiopathic avascular necrosis of the developing femoral head, followed by fragmentation, reossification and remodeling. AGE Usually 4–10 years. More common in boys. ETIOLOGY The exact cause is unknown. Possible factors: Temporary interruption of blood supply, Genetic predisposition, Coagulation abnormalities, Environmental factors, Repeated microtrauma. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Interruption of blood supply ↓ Femoral head ischemia ↓ Bone necrosis ↓ Fragmentation ↓ Revascularization ↓ Reossification ↓ Remodeling STAGES 1. 2. 3. 4. Initial/necrosis stage Fragmentation stage Reossification stage Healed/remodeling stage CLINICAL FEATURES • • Limp, Hip pain, Groin pain, Referred knee pain Reduced hip ROM (Restricted abduction, Restricted internal rotation) Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 10 of 17Muscle wasting, Trendelenburg gait • INVESTIGATIONS • • X-ray: May show increased density of femoral head, flattening, fragmentation, subchondral fracture, loss of sphericity. MRI: Can detect early disease before X-ray changes. COMPLICATIONS • • Femoral head deformity, Coxa magna, Coxa plana, Subluxation Leg-length discrepancy, Early osteoarthritis, Chronic hip pain, Reduced hip function MEDICAL/CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT Goals: Maintain hip mobility, Maintain femoral head containment, Reduce mechanical stress, Prevent deformity. Methods: Activity modification (Avoid high-impact activities), Analgesics/NSAIDs when appropriate, Physiotherapy (ROM exercises, particularly abduction and internal rotation), Traction in selected cases, Bracing/containment treatment in selected patients. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Indications: Depend on age, femoral head involvement, loss of containment, progressive deformity. Procedures: Femoral varus osteotomy, Pelvic osteotomy, Other containment procedures. PHYSIOTHERAPY Gentle ROM, Hip abduction exercises, Muscle strengthening, Hydrotherapy where appropriate, Gait training, Avoid excessive impact, Maintain functional independence. 10. SLIPPED CAPITAL FEMORAL EPIPHYSIS — SCFE DEFINITION Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) is a disorder of adolescence in which the proximal femoral epiphysis slips relative to the femoral neck through the growth plate, usually producing posteromedial displacement of the epiphysis relative to the metaphysis. AGE & RISK FACTORS Age: Commonly 10–16 years, often during the adolescent growth spurt. More common in boys. Risk Factors: Obesity, Rapid growth, Endocrine disorders (Hypothyroidism, Growth hormone abnormalities), Renal disease, Previous radiotherapy. CLASSIFICATION • • Stable SCFE: Patient can walk, with or without crutches. Unstable SCFE: Patient cannot walk even with assistance. (Higher risk of avascular necrosis) CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • Hip pain, Groin pain, Thigh pain, Referred knee pain, Limp Reduced hip movement Characteristic finding: During passive hip flexion, the hip may move into external rotation. Shortened affected limb, External rotation deformity, Limited internal rotation, Difficulty walking. INVESTIGATIONS • • X-ray: AP pelvis, Frog-leg lateral for stable cases (avoid forced positioning in suspected unstable SCFE). May show widened physis, displacement of epiphysis, Klein’s line abnormality. MRI: Useful in early/pre-slip cases when radiographs are inconclusive. Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 11 of 17COMPLICATIONS • • Avascular necrosis of femoral head, Chondrolysis, Femoroacetabular impingement Premature osteoarthritis, Limb deformity, Leg-length discrepancy MEDICAL MANAGEMENT SCFE requires urgent orthopaedic management. Until definitive treatment: Stop weight-bearing, Bed/rest or protected mobility, Analgesia, Avoid forceful hip manipulation. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Standard treatment is in-situ fixation, especially for stable slips (e.g., percutaneous screw fixation). For selected severe/ unstable slips, more extensive corrective procedures may be considered. Prophylactic fixation of contralateral hip may be considered in high-risk patients. PHYSIOTHERAPY After surgical stabilization: Protected weight-bearing as prescribed, ROM exercises as allowed, Progressive strengthening, Gait training, Functional rehabilitation, Weight management and activity modification. 11. AVASCULAR NECROSIS OF BONE DEFINITION Avascular necrosis (AVN) is the death of bone tissue caused by loss or significant reduction of its blood supply. The femoral head is a common site. CAUSES • • Traumatic: Femoral neck fracture, Hip dislocation Non-traumatic: Long-term corticosteroid use, Excessive alcohol use, Sickle-cell disease, Certain autoimmune diseases, Radiation, Caisson disease, Hypercoagulable states, Some metabolic disorders, Idiopathic. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Reduced blood supply ↓ Ischemia ↓ Death of osteocytes ↓ Structural weakening ↓ Subchondral fracture ↓ Collapse of bone ↓ Secondary osteoarthritis CLINICAL FEATURES • • Early: May be asymptomatic, Mild hip/groin pain. Progressive: Deep hip pain, Pain on weight-bearing, Pain at rest in advanced disease, Restricted ROM, Limp, Difficulty climbing stairs, Difficulty walking. INVESTIGATIONS • • • X-ray: May be normal in early disease. Later: Sclerosis, Cystic changes, Crescent sign, Femoral head collapse, Secondary osteoarthritis. MRI: Most sensitive investigation for early AVN. CT: Useful for assessing structural collapse. Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 12 of 17COMPLICATIONS • • Femoral head collapse, Secondary osteoarthritis, Severe pain Reduced mobility, Leg-length discrepancy, Joint stiffness, Disability MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Depends on stage: Activity modification, Protected weight-bearing, Analgesics/NSAIDs, Treatment of underlying cause (reduction/discontinuation of causative drugs only under medical supervision), Management of associated metabolic conditions. Note: No medical treatment reliably reverses established femoral-head collapse. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • Early disease: Core decompression, Bone grafting in selected cases, Joint-preserving procedures. Advanced disease: Total hip arthroplasty if significant collapse and arthritis are present. PHYSIOTHERAPY Pain-relieving modalities as appropriate, Protected weight-bearing, ROM exercises, Strengthening, Gait training, Assistive devices, Postoperative rehabilitation after arthroplasty. PART C — METABOLIC BONE DISEASES 12. RICKETS DEFINITION Rickets is a disorder of the growing skeleton caused by defective mineralization of the growth plate and newly formed bone, most commonly due to vitamin D deficiency or impaired vitamin D metabolism. CAUSES • • • • Nutritional: Vitamin D deficiency, Calcium deficiency, Phosphate deficiency Malabsorption: Chronic gastrointestinal disease, Malabsorption syndromes Renal: Chronic kidney disease, Renal tubular disorders Genetic: Hereditary vitamin D–dependent rickets, Hypophosphatemic rickets PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Vitamin D / calcium / phosphate deficiency ↓ Reduced mineralization ↓ Defective growth plate mineralization ↓ Bone deformity and weakness ↓ Skeletal abnormalities CLINICAL FEATURES General: Growth retardation, Irritability, Muscle weakness, Delayed development. Skeletal: Frontal bossing, Craniotabes in infants, Delayed closure of fontanelle, Rachitic rosary, Harrison sulcus, Pigeon chest, Widening of wrists and ankles, Bow legs, Knock knees, Spinal deformity. Dental: Delayed dentition, Enamel defects. Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 13 of 17INVESTIGATIONS • • Blood: Serum calcium, Phosphate, ALP, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, PTH, Renal function when indicated. X-ray: Typical metaphyseal widening, cupping, fraying, splaying, reduced bone density. COMPLICATIONS • • Skeletal deformity, Growth retardation, Fractures Muscle weakness, Delayed motor development, Hypocalcemic seizures in severe cases, Dental abnormalities MEDICAL MANAGEMENT • • Nutritional rickets: Vitamin D supplementation, Adequate calcium intake, Adequate phosphate where required, Balanced diet, Safe sunlight exposure. Specific forms: Treatment directed at underlying cause (e.g., Phosphate supplementation for hypophosphatemic disorders, Active vitamin D analogues in appropriate disorders). SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Usually unnecessary if treated early. May be required for severe residual deformity or persistent angular deformity (Procedures: Guided growth, Corrective osteotomy). 13. OSTEOMALACIA DEFINITION Osteomalacia is a disorder of adults characterized by defective mineralization of newly formed bone, most commonly due to vitamin D deficiency or impaired vitamin D metabolism. Difference: Rickets affects the growing skeleton and growth plates; osteomalacia affects mature bone. CAUSES Vitamin D deficiency, Calcium deficiency, Malabsorption, Chronic kidney disease, Liver disease affecting vitamin D metabolism, Anticonvulsant medications, Phosphate-wasting disorders. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Vitamin D deficiency / impaired mineral metabolism ↓ Reduced calcium/phosphate availability ↓ Defective mineralization ↓ Accumulation of osteoid ↓ Soft and weak bones CLINICAL FEATURES • • • Diffuse bone pain, Bone tenderness Proximal muscle weakness, Difficulty climbing stairs, Difficulty rising from a chair Waddling gait, Fractures or pseudofractures, Fatigue INVESTIGATIONS • • • Blood: Calcium, Phosphate, ALP, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, PTH, Renal function. X-ray: May show reduced bone density, Looser zones/pseudofractures. Bone density: May show reduced BMD but does not by itself distinguish osteomalacia from osteoporosis. COMPLICATIONS • Pathological fractures, Pseudofractures, Skeletal deformity Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 14 of 17Chronic pain, Muscle weakness, Falls, Functional disability • MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Vitamin D replacement, Calcium supplementation, Correction of phosphate deficiency where applicable, Treatment of underlying malabsorption/renal disease, Nutritional correction. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT & PHYSIOTHERAPY Surgery: Generally not required. May be needed for severe deformity or complicated fractures. Physiotherapy: Muscle strengthening, Balance training, Gait training, Fall prevention, Functional training, Avoid excessive loading until bone strength improves. 14. OSTEOPENIA DEFINITION Osteopenia is a condition in which bone mineral density is lower than normal but not low enough to meet the diagnostic criteria for osteoporosis. For postmenopausal women and men aged 50 years and older, a DXA T-score between −1.0 and −2.5 is generally classified as osteopenia. CAUSES / RISK FACTORS Aging, Inadequate calcium intake, Vitamin D deficiency, Physical inactivity, Smoking, Excessive alcohol, Low body weight, Menopause, Endocrine disorders, Long-term glucocorticoid therapy, Chronic diseases. CLINICAL FEATURES Usually asymptomatic. It may be discovered through bone mineral density testing or evaluation after a fracture. INVESTIGATIONS DXA scan (Main investigation): • • • T-score ≥ −1.0 = normal T-score −1.0 to −2.5 = osteopenia T-score ≤ −2.5 = osteoporosis (In younger adults, Z-scores are generally more appropriate for interpretation.) COMPLICATIONS Progression to osteoporosis, Increased fracture risk (Vertebral fractures, Hip fractures), Reduced physical function. MEDICAL MANAGEMENT Management is based on overall fracture risk, not the T-score alone. Adequate calcium & vitamin D intake, Weightbearing exercise, Resistance training, Smoking cessation, Avoid excessive alcohol, Fall prevention, Treatment of secondary causes, Pharmacological therapy for selected patients with sufficiently high fracture risk. PHYSIOTHERAPY Resistance exercises, Weight-bearing activities, Balance training, Postural exercises, Gait training, Fall prevention, Functional strengthening. 15. OSTEOPOROSIS DEFINITION Osteoporosis is a systemic skeletal disorder characterized by reduced bone strength, resulting from low bone mass and/ or deterioration of bone microarchitecture and consequently an increased risk of fragility fractures. TYPES 1. Primary osteoporosis: Type I (Postmenopausal osteoporosis), Type II (Age-related/senile osteoporosis). Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 15 of 172. Secondary osteoporosis: Due to Endocrine disorders, Chronic kidney disease, Malabsorption, Rheumatological disease, Medications (especially glucocorticoids), Prolonged immobilization, Nutritional disorders. RISK FACTORS • • • Non-modifiable: Increasing age, Female sex, Previous fragility fracture, Family history, Menopause. Modifiable: Smoking, Excessive alcohol, Physical inactivity, Poor calcium/vitamin D intake, Low body weight, Prolonged immobilization. Drug-related: Long-term glucocorticoids, Some anticonvulsants, Certain hormonal therapies. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY Bone resorption exceeds bone formation ↓ Loss of bone mass ↓ Trabecular thinning and cortical porosity ↓ Reduced bone strength ↓ Fragility fracture CLINICAL FEATURES Often called a “silent disease” because bone loss may occur without symptoms until fracture develops. Manifestations: Back pain, Vertebral compression fracture, Loss of height, Kyphosis, Fragility fracture (Hip, Wrist, Vertebrae), Difficulty walking, Reduced independence. INVESTIGATIONS • • • DXA scan: Standard test for bone mineral density. T-score thresholds apply (≥ -1.0 Normal, -1.0 to -2.5 Osteopenia, ≤-2.5 Osteoporosis). Blood investigations: Calcium, Phosphate, ALP, Vitamin D, Renal function, Thyroid studies (to identify secondary causes). X-ray: Fractures, Vertebral compression, Kyphosis. COMPLICATIONS Vertebral compression fracture, Hip fracture, Wrist fracture, Loss of height, Kyphosis, Chronic pain, Reduced mobility, Falls, Loss of independence, Increased mortality following major hip fracture. MEDICAL MANAGEMENT 1. 2. Lifestyle management: Adequate calcium & vitamin D, Weight-bearing & resistance exercise, Balance training, Smoking cessation, Limit excessive alcohol, Fall prevention. Pharmacological treatment: Bisphosphonates, Denosumab, Selective estrogen receptor modulators, Anabolic agents (teriparatide/abaloparatide), Romosozumab. Choice depends on fracture risk, age, renal function, previous fractures, contraindications. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Osteoporosis itself is usually managed medically. Surgery is required for complications (e.g., fracture management via internal fixation, arthroplasty, vertebral augmentation for vertebral compression, hemiarthroplasty/THA for hip fractures). PHYSI